"""
## Purpose and audience
Purpose: Pin that the retained AMBIGDBG import-resolution probe log is SILENT
unless SIMPLE_AMBIGDBG=1, so it can stay in the tree at zero cost.
Audience: compiler engineers maintaining HIR import/re-export materialization.
## Operator workflow
Run this spec with the test runner and read the per-scenario verdict lines.
Enable the trace with `SIMPLE_AMBIGDBG=1` when investigating an
`ambiguous explicit callable dependency` diagnostic.
## Compatibility and limitations
This asserts the GATE, not the absence of bytes on stderr: a spec cannot
capture its own process's stderr, so the pinned contract is that the gate reads
false when the variable is unset and that the sink is a no-op in that state.
Every call site is required to test the gate before building its message,
because interpolation happens at the call site.
# @manual: primary
REQ-COMPILER-HIR-001
doc/01_research/local/REQ-COMPILER-HIR-001.md
doc/03_plan/sys_test/REQ-COMPILER-HIR-001.md
doc/04_architecture/REQ-COMPILER-HIR-001.md
doc/05_design/REQ-COMPILER-HIR-001.md
"""
# @req: REQ-COMPILER-HIR-001

use std.spec.{describe, it, expect}
use compiler.hir.hir_lowering.hir_phase_profile.{hir_ambig_dep_trace, hir_ambig_dep_trace_enabled}

describe("AMBIGDBG import-resolution trace"):
    it("is off unless SIMPLE_AMBIGDBG=1"):
        # oracle: false — default OFF, the retention-policy requirement for a
        # kept probe log
        expect(hir_ambig_dep_trace_enabled()).to_be_falsy()

    it("is a no-op sink while off"):
        hir_ambig_dep_trace("this line must never reach stderr")
        # oracle: false — the gate stays off after a call, i.e. the sink has no
        # side effect that could latch tracing on
        expect(hir_ambig_dep_trace_enabled()).to_be_falsy()
